[PROTOCOL_ANALYSIS]
Manufacturer=Jeep/Chrysler (Stellantis)

Protocols_Used:
- CCD bus (Chrysler Collision Detection) - Proprietary
- SCI bus (Serial Communication Interface) - Proprietary
- CAN bus - Limited standard OBD-II access
- ISO 15765-4 CAN (11 bit, 500 kbaud) - Powertrain only

Standard_OBD_Access=VERY LIMITED
Reason=Most modules use proprietary CCD/SCI protocols

Modules_Accessible_Via_Standard_OBD:
- PCM: Partial (requires extended session)
- Standard OBD-II PIDs: Yes (Mode 01)

Modules_NOT_Accessible_Via_Standard_OBD:
- TCM: No response to UDS commands
- Airbag: Uses CCD/SCI bus
- BCM: Uses CCD/SCI bus
- IPC: Uses CCD bus
- HVAC: Unknown protocol

Security_Access=Likely required for extended session
Authentication=Manufacturer-specific

[ALFAOBD_COMPARISON]
StandardOBD=Very limited access
AlfaOBD=Full access to all modules

StandardOBD_Can_Access:
- Basic DTCs (Mode 03)
- Live data (Mode 01 PIDs)
- VIN (partial/unreliable)
- Emissions readiness (Mode 01 PID 01)

StandardOBD_Cannot_Access:
- Transmission gear position
- Airbag DTCs
- Extended PCM DIDs
- Module programming
- Actuator tests
- Security access

AlfaOBD_Can_Access:
- All PCM DIDs
- Transmission gear position
- Airbag DTCs and crash data
- All module DTCs
- Module programming
- Actuator tests
- Security access handling
- Adaptation values

Cost_Comparison:
StandardOBD=Free (with existing adapter)
AlfaOBD=$50 (one-time)
Recommendation=AlfaOBD is essential for Jeep diagnostics

[MANUFACTURER_COMPARISON]
# Comparison with other manufacturers tested

Ford_Escape_2008:
- Standard OBD access: GOOD
- PCM: Fully accessible
- ABS: Partially accessible
- Transmission: UDS DIDs work
- HVAC: Requires FORScan
- Overall: 70% accessible via standard OBD

Toyota_FJ_Cruiser_2008:
- Standard OBD access: MODERATE
- PCM: Fully accessible
- ABS: Accessible (no response is normal)
- Transmission: Limited
- Overall: 50% accessible via standard OBD

Jeep_Wrangler_JK_2007:
- Standard OBD access: VERY LIMITED
- PCM: Requires extended session
- TCM: Not accessible
- Airbag: Not accessible (CCD/SCI bus)
- Overall: 20% accessible via standard OBD

Conclusion:
Jeep/Chrysler has the most restrictive OBD-II implementation
of the three manufacturers tested. AlfaOBD is essential.

[RECOMMENDATIONS]
For_Basic_Diagnostics:
- Use standard OBD-II for DTCs and live data
- Read VIN from dashboard, not OBD-II
- Accept limited module access

For_Advanced_Diagnostics:
- Purchase AlfaOBD (~$50) - HIGHLY RECOMMENDED
- Use existing ELM327 adapter with AlfaOBD
- Full access to all modules

For_CAN_Bus_Work:
- Upgrade to quality adapter (Vgate, OBDLink)
- Or use dedicated CAN sniffer
- Or use AlfaOBD (easier and more reliable)

For_Professional_Use:
- AlfaOBD for Jeep/Chrysler
- FORScan for Ford
- Techstream for Toyota
- Quality adapter for standard OBD

